  "scope": {
    "rust": "Actual finalization.rs start/next/advance/finished functions have View conformance proofs. Stage precedes standalone Seal, packed mode completes after Stage, failure halts and completion requires all appropriate steps. Root/segment mapping, the I/O dispatch loop, catalog durability and whole publication recovery remain unproved.",
    "tla": "Four finite positive and four algorithm-negative configurations. Starts with durable data and a registered open file; serialized catalog outcomes may persist despite a reported error. GC interleaves. Progress additionally requires caller retries, eventual stable I/O, weak fairness and final release/packed close. Negative controls select TypeOK plus their named consequence. Full byte replay, multi-file record_set composition and backend object reclamation are not proved.",
    "four_host": "Independent pools, 2 KiB and 8 MiB repeated-byte payloads, all three standalone APIs. Healthy old calls may remain unreclaimable after explicit seal, release, close and fresh reopen. Fixed retry faults are callback EIO before/after seal or catalog sync, not actual service crashes. 48 retained baseline fixture files were removed manually only after all remote commands returned, separate from 400 GC deletions.",
    "large": "One real 100 decimal GB native streamed write_inputs call in standalone mode, full-extent checksum authentication via Config::inspect, release and GC. The 8 MiB random pattern repeats; writes are sequential and not sparse. Same-host read with no cache eviction; overlaps the small four-host experiment. Logical payload throughput includes publication work or authentication; it is not a physical-device/object-store bandwidth guarantee. GC measures namespace deletion and receipt persistence, not completion of backend object reclamation.",
    "limits": "No automatic sealing of historical open entries, no automatic caller retry/abort daemon, and no new file-format or payload-size limit. No physical TB test, p99 for the single 100 GB sample, latency guarantee or whole-system bug-freedom claim. Existing read-return allocation and metadata-memory limits remain independent."
  },
